    "task_type": "study_forensics",
    "prompt": "A large-scale study investigated a literacy support program in which elementary school students received weekly supplementary instruction over two school years. Students were randomly assigned to either the program or regular instruction. By the end of the first year, participants' reading proficiency was on average two and a half months ahead of the comparison group, and this advantage was still detectable 26 months later. The researchers note that the effect remained stable across all age groups and that no systematic disadvantages were observed.\n",
    "context": "Randomized controlled trial with clear intervention-control comparison, long-term\nfollow-up, and cautious reporting. The agent should recognize it as solid without\nhallucinating weaknesses.\n",
    "expected_answer": "solid",
    "reference_solution": "A randomized controlled trial of a literacy program shows a 2.5-month reading advantage over controls that persists 26 months later. The study uses random assignment, reports measurable outcomes, and makes no overclaims. This is a clear solid case.\n",
